{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And when you come into the land, etc.</b> Three commandments are stated here: (1) entering the Land, after the manner of their statement (Ketubot 110b) that everyone is elevated to the land of Israel, etc. (2) To plant every food-tree, for the praise of the Land. (3) To observe the ordinance of orlah." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in saying <b>and when you come into the land</b>, that the intention should not be for the gratification of material sensations, but rather the intention of coming into the Land should be for the endearment and desire of the Holy Land which HaShem has chosen, the mountain of HaShem is there. And He said, for the intention of this utterance is not to establish the motive of striving to settle the Land as sufficient, but rather ״you shall plant״ etc. — from this you learn that what He conditioned in the phrase ״into the land״ is in the aspect of the ultimate purpose of thought, that it should be for the exaltedness of the Land in the intellects, not for bodily pleasure." "Or it may be that it alludes in saying <b>food-tree</b> to children of Torah, for they too are called plantings, as Rabbi Yosi said (Shabbat 118b): five acts of relations I performed and five plantings I planted; and they are also called tree, as it is written (Isaiah 65:22): ״כִּימֵי הָעֵץ יְמֵי עַמִּי״, and likewise they interpreted in the Zohar (part 3, 203a) on the verse ״לֹא תַשְׁחִית אֶת עֵצָהּ״ that it speaks of a person, a child of Torah; and HaShem commanded that the primary entering of the Land should be to plant in it a food-tree, which are the children of Torah, for the air of the land of Israel makes wise and purifies the soul (Bava Batra 158b), and a child of Torah is called a food-tree because from him comes sustenance for the soul. Go forth and learn from the deed of Rabbi Akiva ( tractate Kallah Rabbati, chapter 2) who taught Torah to that child whose father had died and was in great distress, and from the day he opened his mouth in words of Torah his father benefited in the supernal world:" "<b>And He said</b> three years <b>shall they be to you arelim</b> — meaning sealed, so that no chirping of Torah should emerge from them, which is the fruit of food (Tanhuma, Kedoshim 14), for until three years a child cannot speak, and in the fourth year when he begins to speak they should teach him to say ״תּוֹרָה צִוָּה לָנוּ״, ״שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and this is called ״קֹדֶשׁ הִלּוּלִים״, and in the fifth year you shall eat its fruit, according to their statement of blessed memory (Avot 5:24): at five years for Scripture, and he begins to be a food-tree." "And Scripture juxtaposed to this the commandment of <b>you shall not eat on the blood</b> — Scripture hinted that one's satisfaction in food should not be on account of blood, which is the physical body and its constitution, but for the intellectual purpose alluded to in food-tree. And Scripture juxtaposed to this also the commandment of <b>you shall not divine</b>, for the ultimate purpose of eating blood is the portion of the serpent, in the mystical secret of (Isaiah 65:25) ״וְנָחָשׁ עָפָר לַחְמוֹ״, and it consumes all physicality of man until it is consumed entirely, and this is what He said — <b>and you shall not practice divination</b>, after the manner of the verse (Job 7:9) ״כָּלָה עָנָן וַיֵּלַךְ כֵּן יוֹרֵד שְׁאוֹל לֹא יַעֲלֶה״, and this verse speaks of one who follows after the material in order to multiply fat and blood."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:33.428536761Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}